There is no LICENSE file, so nothing says how anyone may use pidly. Homebrew
will not take a formula without one and Debian will not either, and a repo
handed to someone else — which is the whole premise — carries no permission
with it.

An owner's decision, not an implementation one. The plausible set for a
single shell script with no dependencies:

- MIT. The default expectation for a tool like this; short, understood
  everywhere, requires the notice be kept.
- ISC. MIT with the redundant words removed. Functionally identical.
- 0BSD. Public-domain-equivalent: no notice to keep, nothing to carry into a
  vendored copy. Fits a script people are meant to copy into a repo.
- Apache-2.0. Adds an explicit patent grant and a NOTICE convention. More
  ceremony than a shell script usually wants, and the file is long.

Worth noting against the format's own premise: people are expected to copy
this script into their own repos and to hand trackers to others. A licence
that survives copying without obligation — 0BSD — matches that; MIT is the
more conventional answer and costs a retained notice.

Done when LICENSE exists, the README says which licence, and the packaging
work in 29 can name it.
